(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-2-acute-vision-loss-vignettes-version-2"]={"title":"Acute Vision Loss Vignettes 2 — CMS I Exam 2","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 68-year-old man with a central retinal artery occlusion asks about his general health risk. What does the lecture emphasise?","choices":["His risk depends on intraocular pressure","His stroke risk is markedly increased","His stroke risk is unchanged","His risk is confined to the other eye"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct; plaque reaching the retinal artery implies more in the carotids. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man with a central retinal artery occlusion describes what remains of his sight. Which pattern does the lecture give?","choices":["Flickering and double vision","An island of vision in the temporal field","A central island with peripheral loss","A curtain lifting after seconds"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, with acuity from counting fingers to light perception. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"A 68-year-old woman notices new flashing lights and floaters. What do these represent?","choices":["A retinal tear","Raised intracranial pressure","Optic nerve inflammation","Venous thrombosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct; the detachment commonly follows a tear or hole. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 39-year-old man has papilledema and later develops disc elevation with blurred margins but no haemorrhages or cotton wool spots. Which phase does this describe?","choices":["Optic nerve cupping","Chronic papilledema","Acute papilledema","The atrophic phase"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct; haemorrhages and cotton wool spots mark the acute phase. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"A 62-year-old man has painless loss of only the lower half of the vision in one eye, with haemorrhages confined to the upper retina. How does this differ from a central retinal vein occlusion?","choices":["A smaller branch vein is blocked","The artery rather than the vein is blocked","Intracranial pressure is raised","The macula is detached"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, so only part of the retina is affected. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 55-year-old woman asks which type of glaucoma is more common. What should you tell her?","choices":["The chronic open-angle form","The acute closed-angle form","Neither occurs after age 40","Only the acute form causes blindness"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, much more common than acute closed-angle. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"A 63-year-old man with angle-closure glaucoma is about to start a medication for overactive bladder. Which counselling point applies?","choices":["Anticholinergic drugs cause optic neuritis","Anticholinergic drugs cause papilledema","Anticholinergic drugs can worsen his glaucoma","Anticholinergic drugs will lower his pressure"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct; the lecture names systemic anticholinergics such as atropine. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"A 74-year-old woman presents 40 minutes after sudden painless monocular vision loss. Why is she treated immediately?","choices":["Retinal damage is irreversible after 8 hours","Damage is reversible at any point","Damage occurs only if both eyes are affected","Retinal damage is irreversible after 90 minutes"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct; this is the window the lecture gives. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"A 77-year-old woman is treated for arteritic optic neuropathy. Which test does the lecture call the gold standard for the underlying arteritis?","choices":["MRI of the orbits","Fluorescein angiography","Temporal artery biopsy","C-reactive protein"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"A 61-year-old woman asks which retinal vascular occlusion is seen more often. What does the lecture state?","choices":["Artery occlusion is more common","They occur equally often","Neither occurs after age 50","Vein occlusion is more common"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, more common than central retinal artery occlusion. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"A 59-year-old woman took an over-the-counter antihistamine and hours later developed a severe unilateral headache, a red eye and vomiting. Her pupil is mid-dilated and her cornea looks cloudy. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Papilledema","Acute angle-closure glaucoma","Optic neuritis","Central retinal vein occlusion"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct; anticholinergic drugs precipitate it. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"A 71-year-old woman has had three episodes of painless monocular blackout, each lasting under a minute. Which source does the lecture name as most common?","choices":["Vitreous haemorrhage","A carotid or cardiac embolus","Optic nerve demyelination","Raised intracranial pressure"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Most commonly a transient ischemic attack of retinal origin. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"A 76-year-old woman has sudden vision loss, scalp tenderness and jaw claudication. Which initial tests does the lecture order?","choices":["Carotid Doppler and echocardiogram","MRI of brain and orbits","Erythrocyte sedimentation rate and C-reactive protein","Lumbar puncture"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, the inflammatory markers used to rule the diagnosis in or out. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"A 62-year-old woman has sudden severe right eye pain, a headache, nausea and vomiting. She sees coloured halos around lights and her cornea looks hazy. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Chronic open-angle glaucoma","Optic neuritis","Amaurosis fugax","Acute angle-closure glaucoma"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Pain, halos and systemic upset with a hazy cornea. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man has acute angle-closure glaucoma. What is happening inside his eye?","choices":["Intracranial pressure compresses the disc","A thrombus occludes the central vein","The iris blocks the drainage circuit","The trabecular meshwork ages abnormally"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and intraocular pressure rises dramatically. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"A 64-year-old woman is newly diagnosed with chronic open-angle glaucoma. Which does the lecture list as first-line?","choices":["Laser peripheral iridotomy","Intravenous mannitol","Anterior chamber paracentesis","Latanoprost"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, with tafluprost and timolol drops. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"A 55-year-old man is diagnosed with non-arteritic anterior ischemic optic neuropathy. What share of anterior ischemic optic neuropathy does this form represent?","choices":["90 to 95 percent","50 to 60 percent","5 to 10 percent","Under 5 percent"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and it presents at 40 to 60 years of age. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man with untreated chronic open-angle glaucoma says he feels as though he is looking down a tube. Which finding does this describe?","choices":["Transient blackout","Loss of peripheral vision first","Loss of central vision first","Loss of colour vision"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct; patients call it tunnel vision, and central sight is preserved longest. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"A 33-year-old woman with optic neuritis has an MRI showing three characteristic demyelinating lesions. What does the lecture say happens next?","choices":["Referral to neurology or neuro-ophthalmology","Reassurance and discharge","Temporal artery biopsy","Lumbar puncture to lower pressure"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, once at least two such lesions are present. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"A 59-year-old man with sudden monocular vision loss and no other neurological findings asks why he is being worked up so aggressively. Which principle did the lecturer stress twice?","choices":["Sudden vision loss is a stroke only with weakness","Sudden vision loss rarely needs referral","Sudden vision loss is usually migraine","Sudden vision loss is a stroke until proven otherwise"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A stroke can present with acute vision loss and nothing else. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"A 61-year-old woman has a painful red eye with a hazy cornea. Which measurement does the lecture make first?","choices":["Cerebrospinal fluid opening pressure","Colour vision testing","Intraocular pressure","Erythrocyte sedimentation rate"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, by tonometry or gonioscopy; expect 40 to 80 mmHg. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 76-year-old man with carotid disease describes vision in one eye going dark instantly two hours ago and not returning. He has no pain. The retina is pale. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Angle-closure glaucoma","Central retinal artery occlusion","Central retinal vein occlusion","Optic neuritis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct; profound painless loss over seconds that does not recover. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"A 24-year-old woman had numbness in one leg last year that resolved. She now has aching behind one eye that worsens when she looks around, with vision fading over two days. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Optic neuritis","Angle-closure glaucoma","Central retinal vein occlusion","Papilledema"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct; the prior neurological episode points to a demyelinating cause. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"A 57-year-old man with non-arteritic anterior ischemic optic neuropathy takes an antihypertensive at bedtime. Which change may be considered?","choices":["Adding a corticosteroid","Moving the dose away from bedtime","Doubling the bedtime dose","Stopping all antihypertensives"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, to avoid nocturnal hypotension, which can worsen the condition. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man describes a curtain descending over the vision of his right eye. It lasted about 40 seconds, there was no pain, and his sight returned completely. He has hypertension and carotid bruits. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Acute angle-closure glaucoma","Optic neuritis","Amaurosis fugax","Central retinal artery occlusion"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Transient monocular loss lasting seconds to minutes, then full recovery. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"A 81-year-old woman has lost vision in one eye over the past day. She has felt unwell for a month with low-grade fever and aching, and her scalp hurts when she brushes her hair. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Arteritic anterior ischemic optic neuropathy","Central retinal artery occlusion","Optic neuritis","Retinal detachment"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct; systemic arteritic symptoms in an elderly woman. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"A 58-year-old African American man has no eye symptoms. Routine screening shows an increased cup-to-disc ratio, splinter haemorrhages at the rim and a bilateral pressure of 26 mmHg. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Papilledema","Optic neuritis","Chronic open-angle glaucoma","Acute angle-closure glaucoma"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Asymptomatic, with optic nerve changes on an open angle. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 68-year-old woman has sudden painless vision loss. Which optic disc appearance does the lecture describe in anterior ischemic optic neuropathy?","choices":["A deeply cupped disc","A normal-appearing disc","A cherry-red macula","A swollen, pale disc"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, with loss of side or central vision. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"A 65-year-old woman has sudden painless loss of a wedge of vision in one eye, with pallor limited to one arterial territory. Which diagnosis fits?","choices":["Central retinal artery occlusion","Branch retinal vein occlusion","Retinal detachment","Branch retinal artery occlusion"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct; a branch is blocked rather than the main trunk. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"A 34-year-old woman has weeks of headache, nausea and vomiting with flickering and intermittent blurred vision. Both optic discs are swollen with blurred margins and engorged veins. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Papilledema","Optic neuritis","Central retinal vein occlusion","Anterior ischemic optic neuropathy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Bilateral disc swelling with signs of raised intracranial pressure. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 44"}]};
